<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Myles Coverdale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1488–<a href="/years/1569">1569</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Miles Coverdale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Myles Coverdale was an English reformer, Bible translator and preacher who in 1535 produced the first printed translation of the complete Bible into Early Modern English, completing the work of William Tyndale. A Cambridge-educated Augustinian friar, he left the order to preach reform and in 1528 fled to the Continent; knowing little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> or Greek, he translated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and German sources and from Tyndale. He oversaw the Great Bible of 1539, and his Psalms are still used in the 1662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Exeter from 1551, he was ejected under Mary I in 1553 and went into exile again, contributing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Bible, before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1559.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/myles-coverdale.jpg" alt="Myles Coverdale" width="330" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myles_Coverdale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Myles Coverdale</b> made the first translation in 1539; Frederick H. Hedge's of 1853 and Thomas Carlyle's became the most popular outside Lutheranism, while North American Lutherans long sang a composite translation from the 1868 Pennsylvania Lutheran Church Book and, more recently, that of the 1978 Lutheran Book of…</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</a></li><li><blockquote>Jerome is associated with three Latin psalters, and in England the first Book of Common Prayer used <b>Myles Coverdale</b>'s prose rendering.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a></li></ul></section>
